: 0, "edit_calls": 1, "ended_on_rejection": false, "error": null, "exec_mode": "local", "failure_class": "solved", "failure_reason": null, "faults_served": 0, "final_answer_complete": true, "final_answer_present": true, "final_answer_source": "json", "fresh_subagents": 0, "harness": "omp", "harness_version": "11.9.0 (/workspace/pi-check/cache2/bin/omp/20663d0e0d0452dc3e41b38370e1e8f6e7036579/omp)", "harness_version_source": "host", "host": "grok-bot-vm-412615941", "image_digest": null, "last_activity_age_s": null, "list_has_generated": null, "main_agent_searched": false, "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "model_context_window": 1000000, "model_max_tokens": 128000, "output_tail": "Root cause: `to_cents` truncates float results, e.g. `19.99 * 100 == 1998.9999999999998`, which becomes `1998`. I'll fix it by converting via the decimal string representation.\npytest isn't installed, so I run the test functions directly:\nThe bug was in `ledger/currency.py`, in `to_cents`. It did `int(amount * 100)`, and `int()` cuts off the fractional part. Floats can't hold these prices exactly, so the result often lands just under a whole number. For example, `19.99 * 100` is `1998.9999999999998`, which became `1998` and lost a cent. That's why 3 of the 4 tests in `tests/test_totals.py` failed; the 10.00 tax case happened to come out exact and passed.\n\n**Fix:** `to_cents` now converts through the price's string form with `Decimal`, so `19.99` is treated as exactly 19.99. It then rounds to whole cents, rounding halves away from zero. No other files changed.\n\n**Verification:** pytest isn't installed here (and there's no `python`, only `python3`), so I ran each test function directly with `python3`. All 4 tests in `tests/test_totals.py` and the one in `tests/test_report.py` passed. I also spot-checked conversions: 19.99 \u2192 1999, 0.29 \u2192 29, 4.35 \u2192 435, 10 \u2192 1000, -0.29 \u2192 -29, 0 \u2192 0. The project's usual check would be `pytest tests` once pytest is installed.\n\n**